, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA; Institute for Machine Learning, Department of Computer Science, ETH Zürich, Zurich, Switzerland; ETH AI Center, ETH Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland; Swiss Institute for Bioinformatics (SIB), Lausanne, Switzerland."}]}}, {"fullName": "Van Allen EM", "lastName": "Van Allen", "firstName": "Eliezer M", "initials": "EM", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-0201-4444"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Medical Oncology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA; Cancer Program, Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA; Division of Medical Sciences, Harvard University, Boston, MA, USA; Parker Institute for Cancer Immunotherapy, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA. Electronic address: eliezerm_vanallen@dfci.harvard.edu."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-0201-4444"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "4", "volume": "43", "journalIssueId": 3930461, "dateOfPublication": "2025 Apr", "monthOfPublication": 4, "yearOfPublication": 2025, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Cancer Cell", "title": "Cancer cell", "essn": "1878-3686", "issn": "1535-6108", "nlmid": "101130617", "isoabbreviation": "Cancer Cell"}, "printPublicationDate": "2025-04-01"}, "pubYear": "2025", "pageInfo": "708-727", "abstractText": "Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly being utilized in cancer research as a computational strategy for analyzing multiomics datasets. Advances in single-cell and spatial profiling technologies have contributed significantly to our understanding of tumor biology, and AI methodologies are now being applied to accelerate translational efforts, including target discovery, biomarker identification, patient stratification, and therapeutic response prediction. Despite these advancements, the integration of AI into clinical workflows remains limited, presenting both challenges and opportunities. This review discusses AI applications in multiomics analysis and translational oncology, emphasizing their role in advancing biological discoveries and informing clinical decision-making. Key areas of focus include cellular heterogeneity, tumor microenvironment interactions, and AI-aided diagnostics. Challenges such as reproducibility, interpretability of AI models, and clinical integration are explored, with attention to strategies for addressing these hurdles. Together, these developments underscore the potential of AI and multiomics to enhance precision oncology and contribute to advancements in cancer care.", "affiliation": "Department of Medical Oncology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA; Institute for Machine Learning, Department of Computer Science, ETH Zürich, Zurich, Switzerland; ETH AI Center, ETH Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland; Swiss Institute for Bioinformatics (SIB), Lausanne, Switzerland.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["research-article", "Review", "Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "R01 CA227388", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Swiss National Science Foundation", "grantId": "205321", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Ambrose Monell Foundation", "grantId": "R01CA227388", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Swiss National Science Foundation", "acronym": "SNSF_", "grantId": "205321_207931", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Swiss National Science Foundation", "grantId": "207931", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "U2C CA233195", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Institutes of Health", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "R01 CA279221", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "R01 CA278980", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "R37 CA222574", "orderI